{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790258775,"nonce":"oiq3zj7uQxJ5eV6JXQiZjD04Yz0=","title":"Python: augmented assignment evaluates its target's container and index twice","message":"Stmt::AugAssign lowered `a[i] op= v` as a read of `a[i]` and a separate store to `a[i]`, each evaluating `a` and `i`: `f()[idx()] += 5` runs the side effects twice (22 events on zypy where CPython has 11), and LU's `A[ii][jj] -= ...` makes four __getitem__ calls per inner operation. The item or attribute read also ran after anything the right-hand side hoisted, so a right-hand side that writes the same slot changed the value read.\n\nFix: the container, index and read are held in temporaries ahead of the statement, then combined and stored through the same holds.","files":null}]}